1
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,840
Last video, I talked about the dot

2
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:12,640
product, showing both the standard

3
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,640
introduction to the topic, as well as a

4
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,520
deeper view of how it relates to linear

5
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,760
transformations.

6
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:20,080
I'd like to do the same thing for cross

7
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:21,760
products, which also have a standard

8
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:23,680
introduction along with a deeper

9
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,240
understanding in the light of linear

10
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:27,160
transformations, but this time I'm

11
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:29,680
dividing it into two separate videos.

12
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:31,360
Here, I'll try to hit the main points

13
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:32,960
that students are usually shown about

14
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,600
the cross product, and in the next

15
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:36,760
video, I'll be showing a view which is

16
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:38,840
less commonly taught, but really

17
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:40,840
satisfying when you learn it. We'll

18
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:42,880
start in two dimensions. If you have two

19
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:45,440
vectors, V and W, think about the

20
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:48,000
parallelogram that they span out. What I

21
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,080
mean by that is that if you take a copy

22
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:53,280
of V and move its tail to the tip of W,

23
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,040
and you take a copy of W and move its

24
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,520
tail to the tip of V, the four vectors

25
00:00:57,520 --> 00:00:59,640
now on the screen enclose a certain

26
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:01,920
parallelogram.

27
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:04,360
The cross product of V and W, written

28
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:06,800
with the X-shaped multiplication symbol,

29
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:10,840
is the area of this parallelogram.

30
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:12,680
Well, almost. We also need to consider

31
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:15,400
orientation. Basically, if V is on the

32
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:18,640
right of W, then V cross W is positive

33
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:19,880
and equal to the area of the

34
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:21,520
parallelogram.

35
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:23,880
But if V is on the left of W, then the

36
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:25,920
cross product is negative, namely the

37
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,560
negative area of that parallelogram.

38
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:31,000
Notice, this means that order matters.

39
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,800
If you swapped V and W, instead taking W

40
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,840
cross V, the cross product would become

41
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:38,960
the negative of whatever it was before.

42
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:40,480
The way I always remember the ordering

43
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:42,120
here is that when you take the cross

44
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,160
product of the two basis vectors in

45
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:46,840
order, I hat cross J hat, the result

46
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:49,160
should be positive. In fact, the order

47
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:51,240
of your basis vectors is what defines

48
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:52,720
orientation.

49
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:54,640
So, since I hat is on the right of J

50
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:57,480
hat, I remember that V cross W has to be

51
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,440
positive whenever V is on the right of

52
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:01,560
W.

53
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:03,440
So, for example, with the vectors shown

54
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,440
here, I'll just tell you that the area

55
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:07,840
of that parallelogram is seven. And

56
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:10,360
since V is on the left of W, the cross

57
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:12,400
product should be negative. So, V cross

58
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:15,840
W is -7.

59
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:17,360
But, of course, you want to be able to

60
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:19,080
compute this without someone telling you

61
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:20,440
the area.

62
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,840
This is where the determinant comes in.

63
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:24,640
So, if you didn't see chapter five of

64
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:25,959
this series where I talk about the

65
00:02:25,959 --> 00:02:28,000
determinant, now would be a really good

66
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,200
time to go take a look. Even if you did

67
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:31,760
see it, but it was a while ago, I'd

68
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,320
recommend taking another look just to

69
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:34,680
make sure those ideas are fresh in your

70
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:37,160
mind.

71
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:40,080
For the 2D cross product, V cross W,

72
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:41,880
what you do is you write the coordinates

73
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,360
of V as the first column of a matrix,

74
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,040
and you take the coordinates of W and

75
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:47,800
make them the second column. Then, you

76
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,280
just compute the determinant.

77
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,360
This is because a matrix whose columns

78
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:55,640
represent V and W corresponds with a

79
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:57,920
linear transformation that moves the

80
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:00,880
basis vectors I hat and J hat to V and

81
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,440
W.

82
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,480
The determinant is all about measuring

83
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,040
how areas change due to a

84
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:12,320
transformation. And the prototypical

85
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:14,640
area that we look at is the unit square

86
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:17,400
resting on I hat and J hat. After the

87
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:19,600
transformation, that square gets turned

88
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:21,400
into the parallelogram that we care

89
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,640
about. So, the determinant, which

90
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:25,360
generally measures the factor by which

91
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:27,880
areas are changed, gives the area of

92
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:30,239
this parallelogram since it evolved from

93
00:03:30,239 --> 00:03:32,680
a square that started with area one.

94
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:35,600
What's more, if V is on the left of W,

95
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,480
it means that orientation was flipped

96
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,440
during that transformation, which is

97
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:41,000
what it means for the determinant to be

98
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,600
negative.

99
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:46,080
As an example, let's say V has

100
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:49,560
coordinates -3 1, and W has coordinates

101
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:52,840
2 1. The determinant of the matrix those

102
00:03:52,840 --> 00:03:56,840
coordinates as columns is -3 * 1

103
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:59,600
2 * 1,

104
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,560
which is -5.

105
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,160
So, evidently, the area of the

106
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,760
parallelogram they define is five. And

107
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,120
since V is on the left of W, it should

108
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,240
make sense that this value is negative.

109
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,320
As with any new operation you learn, I'd

110
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:14,600
recommend playing around with this

111
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:16,079
notion a bit in your head just to get

112
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:17,640
kind of an intuitive feel for what the

113
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:19,720
cross product is all about.

114
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,239
For example, you might notice that when

115
00:04:21,239 --> 00:04:23,120
two vectors are perpendicular, or at

116
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:25,000
least close to being perpendicular,

117
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,640
their cross product is larger than it

118
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:28,560
would be if they were pointing in very

119
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:31,080
similar directions, because the area of

120
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:33,560
that parallelogram is larger when the

121
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:37,120
sides are closer to being perpendicular.

122
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:38,600
Something else you might notice is that

123
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,160
if you were to scale up one of those

124
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:43,600
vectors, perhaps multiplying V by three,

125
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,120
then the area of that parallelogram is

126
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,960
also scaled up by a factor of three.

127
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:50,840
So, what this means for the operation is

128
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:54,520
that 3V cross W will be exactly three

129
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:58,120
times the value of V cross W.

130
00:04:58,120 --> 00:04:59,520
Now, even though all of this is a

131
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:01,800
perfectly fine mathematical operation,

132
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,200
what I just described is technically not

133
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,360
the cross product. The true cross

134
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:08,360
product is something that combines two

135
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:11,240
different 3D vectors to get a new 3D

136
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:12,520
vector.

137
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:14,000
Just as before, we're still going to

138
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,200
consider the parallelogram defined by

139
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:17,640
the two vectors that we're crossing

140
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:18,880
together.

141
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:20,760
And the area of this parallelogram is

142
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:22,760
still going to play a big role. To be

143
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:25,280
concrete, let's say that the area is 2.5

144
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:27,160
for the vectors shown here.

145
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:28,960
But as I said, the cross product is not

146
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,040
a number, it's a vector.

147
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:33,000
This new vector's length will be the

148
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
area of that parallelogram, which in

149
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,040
this case is 2.5.

150
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:39,080
And the direction of that new vector is

151
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:40,960
going to be perpendicular to the

152
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:42,560
parallelogram.

153
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,640
But which way, right? I mean, there are

154
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,240
two possible vectors with length 2.5

155
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:50,560
that are perpendicular to a given plane.

156
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:52,000
This is where the right-hand rule comes

157
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:52,920
in.

158
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:54,920
Point the forefinger of your right hand

159
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:56,440
in the direction of V.

160
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:58,000
Then stick out your middle finger in the

161
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,680
direction of W.

162
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:01,680
Then, when you point up your thumb,

163
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:02,920
that's the direction of the cross

164
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:05,560
product.

165
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:10,080
For example, let's say that V was a

166
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:12,040
vector with length two pointing straight

167
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:14,440
up in the Z direction, and W is a vector

168
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,560
with length two pointing in the pure Y

169
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:17,840
direction.

170
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:19,520
The parallelogram that they define in

171
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:21,000
this simple example is actually a

172
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,880
square, since they're perpendicular and

173
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:24,800
have the same length. And the area of

174
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,000
that square is four. So, their cross

175
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,440
product should be a vector with length

176
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:29,720
four.

177
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:31,560
Using the right-hand rule, their cross

178
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:33,240
product should point in the negative X

179
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:35,840
direction.

180
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:37,240
So, the cross product of these two

181
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:41,760
vectors is -4 * I-hat.

182
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:47,840
For more general computations, there is

183
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:49,520
a formula that you could memorize if you

184
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:51,240
wanted, but it's common and easier to

185
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,000
instead remember a certain process

186
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,600
involving the 3D determinant.

187
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:58,080
Now, this process looks truly strange at

188
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,680
first. You write down a 3D matrix where

189
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:03,080
the second and third columns contain the

190
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:05,919
coordinates of V and W. But for that

191
00:07:05,919 --> 00:07:07,840
first column, you write the basis

192
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:11,440
vectors I-hat, J-hat, and K-hat.

193
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:13,560
Then, you compute the determinant of

194
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,160
this matrix.

195
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:17,200
The silliness is probably clear here.

196
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:18,720
What on earth does it mean to put in a

197
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:22,520
vector as the entry of a matrix?

198
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:24,000
Students are often told that this is

199
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,960
just a notational trick. When you carry

200
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:28,560
out the computations as if I-hat, J-hat,

201
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:30,480
and K-hat were numbers, then you get

202
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:32,400
some linear combination of those basis

203
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,960
vectors.

204
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:38,080
And the vector defined by that linear

205
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,040
combination, students are told to just

206
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:42,040
believe, is the unique vector

207
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:44,680
perpendicular to V and W whose magnitude

208
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:45,960
is the area of the appropriate

209
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,240
parallelogram and whose direction obeys

210
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:51,200
the right-hand rule.

211
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:53,520
And sure, in some sense, this is just a

212
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:55,800
notational trick, but there is a reason

213
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:57,840
for doing it.

214
00:07:57,840 --> 00:07:59,360
It's not just a coincidence that the

215
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,880
determinant is once again important. And

216
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:03,920
putting the basis vectors in those slots

217
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:06,160
is not just a random thing to do.

218
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:07,640
To understand where all of this comes

219
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:09,640
from, it helps to use the idea of

220
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:11,520
duality that I introduced in the last

221
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:12,680
video.

222
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:14,040
This concept is a little bit heavy,

223
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:16,080
though. So, I'm putting it in a separate

224
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:17,960
follow-on video for any of you who are

225
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:19,560
curious to learn more.

226
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:22,000
Arguably, it falls outside the essence

227
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,840
of linear algebra. The important part

228
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:25,640
here is to know what that cross product

229
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:27,919
vector geometrically represents.

230
00:08:27,919 --> 00:08:29,360
So, if you want to skip that next video,

231
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:31,360
feel free. But for those of you who are

232
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:33,000
willing to go a bit deeper and who are

233
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,440
curious about the connection between

234
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:36,120
this computation and the underlying

235
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:38,159
geometry, the ideas that I'll talk about

236
00:08:38,159 --> 00:08:40,080
in the next video are just a really

237
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:43,240
elegant piece of math.